# CAM-Sim: A Reproducible Benchmark for Situational-Awareness Ablations in Marketing Agents
## Benchmark Paper

> **TL;DR (1 page):** CAM-Sim is a reproducible paired-seed benchmark (50 seeds × 200 scenarios)
> for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents. Multi-signal situation classification
> (intent + competitive density + channel quality) raises match from 87.5% to 98.7% and profit
> from +$155 to +$205 (+$50, p = 8.5e-32). The gain is **signal-driven, not
> classifier-capacity-driven** (confirmed across NCC, logistic regression, and random forest).
> A context-blind policy search reveals a channel-cost arbitrage (cheapest channel dominates
> when reward is channel-independent). The advantage replicates across 9 environments,
> survives 30% label noise and budget pacing, and is checked by paired-difference bootstrap
> (B = 2,000), macro-F1, and common random numbers. All profits are scaled-reward proxies.
> Online learners with a reward-feedback loop recover up to 83% of the fixed-mapping value
> without any labeled action data (§8.18); under mid-episode distribution drift, recalibration
> to the post-shift distribution is net-negative — multi-signal inference is the drift-robust
> remedy (§8.19). Zero-shot API LLM classifiers (temperature 0, qualitative definitions only)
> land at 74–83% raw accuracy — profit-competitive with the hand-tuned classifier (four of five
> beat it on profit) but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), with the
> same emergent 4-of-6 blind spots (from a LAN-local model to a 128B dense model) — confirming
> that labels, not prompting, close the gap (§8.21); zero-shot LLM classification is balanced-inferior and prior-inflated (macro 50–61% vs 65.9%; profit beats only the handicapped single-signal classifier, 610/630-way corrected), while end-to-end LLM action selection (v1.6) exceeds the supervised multi-signal classifier without any fitted component (+$250–273 vs +$206–220).
> **Practical takeaway:** invest first in multi-signal situation
> classification, then in mechanism-calibrated bidding — never in unvalidated bid heuristics.


> **File:** `g4_academic_paper.md`  
> **Status:** DRAFT (v1.6); snapshot 2026-11-03 — results auto-generated from the CAM-Sim benchmark (v0.4 core + v1.3/v1.4/v1.5/v1.6 extensions); peer-reviewed (5 rounds: stats-audit, citation-audit, threats-to-validity, 5× journal-editor, 5× adversarial-reviewer, 5× domain-expert, 5× ml-methodology); all numbers regenerate from cam_sim.py, cam_sim_ablations.py, cam_sim_extensions.py, cam_sim_v14.py, cam_sim_v15_llm.py, cam_sim_v16_arms.py, and results/cam_sim_v16.md  
> **Target:** *NeurIPS Datasets & Benchmarks* / *KDD* / *Journal of Interactive Marketing* / *Journal of Marketing Analytics* (reframed as benchmark paper per round-3 reviewer consensus: JM/MS requires field data; computational venues reward reproducible benchmarks)  
> **Type:** Empirical (Reproducible Benchmark + Ablation Study)  
> **Word Target:** 8,000-10,000 words  
> **Key Contribution:** A reproducible paired-seed benchmark (CAM-Sim) for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 11-agent ladder, 9 environments, matched-family ablation (same NCC classifier, 1 vs 3 signals, +$103.85 signal-driven gap), standard classifier baselines (LR + RF on 1 and 3 signals — multi-signal gain confirmed across 3 classifier families: NCC +$103.85, LR +$65.48, RF +$75.98), context-blind policy search (channel-cost arbitrage: EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3 = +$252 beats every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — §8.7), macro-F1 per-class metric, CRN check, paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000); v1.3: adversarial-mapping condition (0.4% match, -$269), channel-dependent reward (arbitrage fixed), bandit baselines (epsilon-greedy, LinUCB), class-conditional noise, convergence trend (Spearman + Geweke), perception dose-response (rho=0.998), crisis bid policy (pause > counter), sensing fidelity (identical); v1.4: online reward-feedback learning (UCB1 recovers 83.0% of the fixed-mapping value with zero labeled action data), mid-episode distribution drift (post-drift recalibration is net-negative; multi-signal inference is drift-robust), flat-taxonomy confusion quantification (intent-only 11.0% cross-axis confusion vs 1.0% multi-signal); v1.5: LLM zero-shot classification baseline (§8.21) — five models across five providers (incl. a university-hosted 128B Mistral and a LAN-local reasoning model) at temperature 0 reach 74–83% accuracy at $0 API cost, profit-competitive with the hand-tuned single-signal classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), all losing to 2,000-label supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots (never predicting crisis/retention) mirroring the hand-tuned classifier's structural handicap  
**Caveat:** The "first" claim rests on a title/abstract keyword search of our corpus of over 15,700 papers (snapshot 2026-11-02), not a systematic review; contextual-bandit and context-aware-recommender literatures are adjacent but use different terminology (§5.3, §9.2 #13)  

