# Agentic AI and Contextual Intelligence in Marketing
## A Systematic Review of the Construct Gap (WIP)

> **File:** `lit_review_agentic_contextual.md`
> **Status:** DRAFT v0.6 — 10 disciplinary lenses applied; second-coder κ screen complete (κ = 1.00, 18/18); standalone submission (IJRM/JBR) + related-work backbone for CAM-Sim & 2027 WP
> **Purpose:** (a) substantiate the near-zero-intersection claim that motivates the CAM/G4 program (resolves the §5.3 caveat in `g4_academic_paper.md`), (b) anchor the G1 construct-definition gap and the G6 B2B line, (c) feed the related-work section of the CAM-Sim paper (§5.3) and the 2027 Working Paper
> **Evidence base:** this repo's corpus (15,000+ papers; snapshot 2026-10-31 — counts below are live lower bounds, the corpus only grows) + externally verified anchors
> **Lead Author:** Tobias Weiss (proposed) + [TBD co-authors]
> **Target Venues:** *Journal of Business Research*, *Industrial Marketing Management*, *International Journal of Research in Marketing* (review track), or as the related-work backbone of the 2027 Working Paper
> **Last Updated:** 2026-10-29

---

## TL;DR

Agentic AI is entering marketing research fast (≥100 corpus papers, all 2025–2026) but almost entirely as *capability demonstrations* — agents that shop, sell, price, and run campaigns. Contextual intelligence, meanwhile, exists in marketing mainly as *advertising targeting* (contextual ads, context-aware attribution) — a narrow, channel-level usage. At the intersection there are 18 lexical co-mentions, of which only 2 are substantive (3–4 if machine-customer/buyer-side agents are counted — a construct-scope sensitivity documented in Appendix A), and **none defines a marketing construct for agent-level situational/contextual intelligence**. The construct exists only outside marketing: in Endsley's human-factors situational-awareness tradition, in CS/NLP (Häglund 2025), and in B2B sales research as a *human* competence (Böhm et al. 2020 — value opportunity recognition). The review surfaces five gaps and maps each to the repo's research program.

---

**Contribution.** This review makes three contributions: (1) it provides the first corpus-bounded systematic screen of the agentic × contextual intersection in marketing (≤18 papers, 2 substantive); (2) it refines the "zero intersection" claim to a precise construct-level gap (no paper defines what a marketing agent should know about its context, or how that knowledge should be structured); (3) it identifies the theoretical anchors (Endsley, Böhm et al.) that the CAM/G4 program synthesizes into a marketing construct — converting a rhetorical gap into a researchable, well-grounded agenda. The review is designed to serve as the related-work backbone for both the CAM-Sim benchmark paper and the 2027 Working Paper.

## 1. Motivation & Research Questions

The corpus trend data shows two curves rising simultaneously: **agentic** (the corpus's highest-momentum trend, ≥100 papers, all from 2025–2026) and **contextual** (≥250 papers mentioning "contextual", strongly concentrated in 2024–2026). The paper program (CAM/G4, B2B-CI/G6, the 2027 Working Paper) rests on the claim that these two streams have **not yet been joined by a marketing construct**.

This review tests that claim and synthesizes both streams.

**Regulatory boundaries.** Agentic marketing systems that sense and act on contextual data operate under emerging regulatory constraints: the EU AI Act (high-risk AI systems, transparency requirements), GDPR (data minimization, purpose limitation, automated decision-making rights under Art. 22), and consumer protection law (unfair commercial practices). The corpus's privacy-data category (475 papers) treats these as *constraints on data use*, but no paper examines how regulatory boundaries should shape an agent's *situational-awareness model* — e.g., which context dimensions an agent may legally sense, or how consent status should gate context collection. The construct should arguably include a "regulatory boundary" layer. This is adjacent to Gap 1 and Gap 4 (measurement).

**Positioning vs. existing marketing context research.** Marketing already "handles context" in several mature streams — market orientation (Jaworski & Kohli, 1993), market sensing (Day, 1994), customer-journey mapping (Lemon & Verhoef, 2016), and customer-data platforms. The claim here is not that marketing ignores context; it is that none of these streams defines what a *marketing agent* should know about its situation — they are human-organizational or channel-level constructs, not agent-level situational-awareness models. The review therefore distinguishes "context as organizational capability" (well-studied) from "context as agent capability" (absent).

**Ethics and responsible AI.** The review's construct gap has an ethical dimension that the literature does not address: agentic marketing systems that sense and act on context raise questions of consumer consent (what context may an agent use?), manipulation (when does context-aware persuasion cross into exploitation?), transparency (should consumers know an agent is acting on situational signals?), and fairness (do context-aware systems disadvantage certain consumer segments?). The corpus's ethics-related papers (AI ethics in marketing, privacy-data category) treat these as *constraints on targeting*, not as *dimensions of the agent's own situational awareness*. A complete marketing construct for agent-level contextual intelligence should arguably include an ethical boundary layer — what context an agent should *not* use — but no paper in the corpus approaches this. This is a sixth gap (see §9 #6).

**RQ1:** How is agentic AI entering marketing research — as what phenomena, in which roles?
**RQ2:** How is contextual intelligence / context-awareness treated in marketing research — as what construct, at what level?
**RQ3:** What exists at the *intersection* (agents + context), inside marketing and in adjacent fields?
**RQ4:** What does the B2B sales literature contribute on context (value-based selling, value opportunity recognition)?

---

## 2. Method (PRISMA-lite)

**Corpus.** The repo's curated corpus: 15,000+ papers (15,773 at snapshot 2026-10-31), auto-discovered via taxonomy-aware arXiv/OpenAlex/Crossref/dblp queries, each entry carrying one category (11 marketing domains) and one lens (empirical / framework / review / case-study / theory / measurement). Full list in `PAPERS.md`; machine-readable in `docs/papers.json`.

**Search & screening.** Title/abstract keyword searches over the corpus:
- `agentic` → ≥100 papers (167 at snapshot; all 2025–2026); of these, ≥60 (64) also mention sales/lead/B2B/CRM/selling/buyer
- `contextual intelligence` OR `context-aware`/`context aware` → ≥25 papers (27)
- intersection of both → ≤18 papers, using broadened agent vocabulary (`agentic` OR `multi-agent` OR `autonomous agent` OR `ai agent` OR `llm agent`; agent-broad pool 384, context pool 380), screened individually (§5)
- `value opportunity` → 0 papers

**Inclusion.** Papers whose *substance* (not just vocabulary) addresses (a) agents acting in marketing/sales roles, (b) context as a targeting/decision input in marketing, or (c) both. Preprints (arXiv, SSRN, Research Square, Zenodo) are included but flagged as non-peer-reviewed.

**Exclusion.** Papers mentioning both terms only lexically (e.g., an unrelated "agent" such as chemical agents, or "context" in a passage) without substantive engagement.

**Method justification (why "PRISMA-lite").** The corpus is auto-ingested and corpus-bounded; a full PRISMA flow (database-independent search across WoS/Scopus) is not feasible within the corpus's design. The review therefore follows PRISMA *screening* discipline (explicit eligibility, documented counts, PRISMA flow diagram in `paper/prisma_flow.svg`) but not PRISMA *search* (no database-independent search). This is a deliberate scope choice: the review's construct-gap claim is framed as "no construct in a 15,000+ paper marketing corpus" (§12 #1), not as global absence. **Screen limitation:** "value opportunity" → 0 is a single-keyword screen; the construct might appear as "value recognition", "opportunity sensing", or "value opportunity identification". The 0-count is a lower bound on the construct's *visibility*, not proof of its absence.

**Honest caveats.** (1) This is a *corpus-bounded* review: it covers what this curated corpus contains, not the full literature — useful for construct-gap claims within the corpus's coverage, not as proof of global absence. (2) Counts are lower bounds tied to a snapshot (2026-10-31); the corpus grows weekly. (3) The agentic stream is less than two years old; much of it sits in preprints and low-tier venues — quality varies widely. (4) **Vocabulary noise.** "Agentic" is used loosely in the corpus: the 167-title/abstract matches include false positives (chemical agents, stock-trading agents, gaming agents) alongside genuine marketing/sales agents. The sub-stream analysis (§3.1–3.3) was hand-screened for substance, but the headline count (≥100) is a lexical upper bound, not a precise population count. (5) **Vocabulary inconsistency.** The base rate uses "agentic" only; the intersection screen uses five agent terms (agentic/multi-agent/autonomous/ai agent/llm agent). This broadening captures context-aware agent papers that avoid the word "agentic", but it also widens the denominator — the "18 lexical co-mentions" is an upper bound on the intersection, and the true substantive count (2) is the conservative figure. (6) **Venue-quality distribution.** The corpus's top venues by count are Zenodo (1,152), SSRN (149), and book series — the agentic stream inherits this distribution (most matches are preprints or low-tier venues). The review weights the peer-reviewed anchors (*JBR* 2026-01, *RaFT* 2026-09, *Applied Sciences* 2026-09) more heavily than preprints, but the overall quality of the agentic literature is lower than the corpus average. (7) **Bibliometric depth.** This review uses keyword screening only; co-citation analysis, bibliographic coupling, or topic modeling could strengthen (or challenge) the gap claim. These are deferred to the 2027 Working Paper (RQ H7).

---

## 3. Theme A — Agentic AI Entering Marketing (RQ1)

Three sub-streams, all emerging 2025–2026:

### 3.1 The consumer side: agentic shopping and machine customers
- *Shopping by algorithm: How agentic AI deploys human heuristics as a surrogate consumer* (Wadi & Ma, arXiv, 2026-09) — agents as consumer surrogates.
- *When AI Shops for Us: A Systematic Literature Review on Agentic AI and the Algorithmic Consumer* (OPUS BW, 2026-09, preprint) — first corpus SLR on the agentic-consumer phenomenon.
- *When the customer is a machine: a systematic literature review and integrative framework* (Moghaddam, Alipour & Grunert, Research Square, 2026-10, preprint) — machine customers as a new research object; the most explicit signal that the *counterparty* of marketing is becoming an agent.
- *Marketing to Machines: Strategic Frameworks for Engagement with Autonomous Agentic AI* (book chapter, 2026-10) — strategy frameworks aimed at machine audiences.

**Reading:** a coherent, fast-forming conversation — marketing's target shifts from humans to agents, with implications for segmentation, persuasion, and measurement.

**Consumer-psychological dimension (underdeveloped).** The "machine customer" stream (§3.1) raises consumer-behavior questions that the corpus does not yet address: How do human consumers *trust* an agent acting on their behalf? What happens to consumer *agency* when purchase decisions are delegated to agents? Does agent-mediated consumption reduce or amplify reactance? The consumer-behavior literature on trust calibration, anthropomorphism, and consumer autonomy (well-represented in the corpus's consumer-behavior category, 1,731 papers) has not been applied to agentic consumption. This is a research opportunity adjacent to Gap 1.

### 3.2 The firm side: agents in sales, CRM, and marketing operations
- *AI agents, agentic AI, and the future of sales* (Gonzalez, Habel & Hunter) — *Journal of Business Research* (2026-01). The strongest peer-reviewed anchor in the corpus: positions agents across the sales function.
- *The Augmented Rep: Agentic AI Across the Enterprise Sales Cycle* (IRJAEM, 2026-06, preprint) — stage-by-stage agent augmentation of the sales cycle.
- *Agentic Agent, Better Agent: Evidence from Agentic AI in Telemarketing* (SSRN working paper, 2026-04) — rare *empirical* evidence on agentic (vs. generative) AI effects.
- *The Dual-Journey Model of Marketing in the Agentic Era* (SSRN, 2026-01) — conceptualizes parallel human/agent journeys.
- *GENIE CRM: An Agentic AI-Powered Customer Relationship Management System* (ISJEM, 2026-04, preprint) — system-level design artifact.
- *Calibrating Professional Discretion in Artificial Intelligence-Supported B2B Selling* (RaFT, 2026-09) — human discretion vs. agent automation in B2B selling.
- *From Data Quality to Quality of Agentic Data Use: A Conceptual Framework* (Applied Sciences, 2026-09).

### 3.3 Infrastructure: benchmarks, metrics, and governance
- *GTM-Bench: Evaluating Agentic AI Systems for Buyer/Seller Coherence in Go-to-Market Workflows* (technical report, 2026-09) — first go-to-market agent benchmark in the corpus.
- *Can AI Agents Simulate A/B Test Outcomes? A Validation Framework for Agentic Experimentation* (arXiv, 2026-08).
- *AI Win-Rate: A Board Metric for AI-Mediated Buying (Methodology v1.0)* (Zenodo, 2026-09, preprint).
- *AI, Agents, and the Marketing Operating System* (Routledge, 2026-10) — the "marketing OS" framing.
- *Privacy Preference Controls for Agentic Systems* (arXiv, 2026-10) — governance of agent behavior.

**IS/technology-adoption perspective.** The review's "agents demonstrated, not contextualized" gap (Gap 2) has an organizational-readiness dimension that the IS literature addresses but marketing does not: how do organizations *adopt* agentic marketing AI? The corpus's digital-marketing and analytics categories contain technology-adoption studies (TAM/UTAUT-based), but none applies adoption theory to *contextual* (vs. generic) agentic systems. Key open questions: Does organizational readiness for agentic AI differ when the system requires multi-signal context integration? How does the "capability trap" (§3.3 So-what) affect adoption decisions? The IS literature on human-in-the-loop systems and IT-business alignment (well-developed outside this corpus) is relevant but not engaged.

**Forward-snowball (outside the corpus).** Citations of the JBR anchor (Semantic Scholar, 2026-10-31) show the field consolidating in top sales journals: a *JBR* field consolidation of AI in sales (2017–early 2026, 2027), and *JPSSM* papers on AI maturity in buyer–seller exchange and salesperson AI wisdom along the B2B buying journey. The agentic conversation is entering peer-reviewed sales research faster than the corpus's ingestion reflects — the corpus-bounded screen undercounts the peer-reviewed core (see §12 #1).

**Reading (RQ1 answer).** Agentic AI enters marketing as (i) a *new customer type* (machine customers), (ii) a *workforce layer* (sales/CRM agents), and (iii) *infrastructure* (benchmarks, metrics, governance). What it is **not**, anywhere in the corpus: a treatment of what agents need to *know about marketing context* in order to act well. Capability is demonstrated; the informational substrate — the situational context that makes an action appropriate — is assumed.

**Synthesis (RQ1):** The agentic stream is a capability-first literature. It demonstrates what agents *can do* (shop, sell, price, govern) but assumes the informational substrate they need to act appropriately. This confirms the gap is not merely a lack of papers, but a lack of conceptualization of agent-level contextual intelligence.

**So what for practice.** Practitioners deploying agentic AI today (sales agents, shopping agents, pricing agents) are operating without a theory of what their agent needs to *know* about the marketing situation. The result: agents that act confidently on incomplete context — the "capability trap" where better execution amplifies the cost of wrong situational judgment. Before investing in more agentic capability, invest in mu
